          "id": "note",
          "kind": "note",
          "data": {
            "text": "## Beam and stitch\n\nTwo things you can do with coherent lanes that one radio cannot.\n\n**Beamformer** combines the lanes so the array listens hardest in one direction. Its `steer` wire comes from the **Direction finder**, so it aims itself at whatever the DF currently sees: find the signal, then point at it, without turning anything by hand. Set `mode` to null instead and it does the opposite, putting a null on an interferer.\n\nThe beam leaves as one clean lane, so a **Channel** decodes it exactly as it would a plain radio.\n\n**Stitch** uses the same lanes for the other purpose: tune them to adjacent slices and it joins them into one band wider than any single radio could sample. The **Scope** on its output shows the joined span, with the seams flattened.\n\nYou cannot do both at full rate at once. Delete the half you are not using."
          },
          "position": { "x": 0.0, "y": 520.0 }
        }
